//
// A closure record ADOPTS the store behind a capture its defining frame owns: the frame
// emits no scope-exit free, and `free_named`'s cascade reclaims the store when the record
// dies.  That is what lets a returned closure keep its captures (`(L-Escape)`, loft#1308)
// and what stops a captured PARAMETER being freed under its still-living caller (#682).
//
// The rule has THREE consumers — the free emitter (`get_free_vars`), the static leak assert
// that mirrors it (`check_ref_leaks`), and `ownership_cfg`'s leak oracle — and they have to
// answer identically.  A suppression the mirror does not know about reads as a leak; a leak
// the mirror excuses reads as nothing at all.  Every time one of them has been written out
// longhand they have drifted, so all three now ask `capture_adoption_owns_free`.
//
// What this file locks is the disjunction, not just the shape test.  **A capture names ONE
// local, but the store is not always in it.**  A struct or a keyed collection owns its store
// outright — `h: hash(726) OWNS`, no backing local — and the capture itself is the owner, so
// `is_captured(v)` alone reaches it.  A vector local is a VIEW: `v: vec<int> deps=[__vdb_1]`
// beside `__vdb_1: ref(467) OWNS`, and the owner is a local NO CLOSURE CAPTURED BY NAME.
// Reaching it takes the second half, `backs_an_adopted_capture`.  The mirror kept only the
// first half, so every captured vector below reported its backing local as an unfreed leak.
//
// Axes it MOVES: capture heap kind (vector / hash / sorted / index / spatial / struct / text /
//   record enum),
//   how the collection was built (literal, empty-then-appended, call result), closure fate
//   (kept local, returned, stored in a struct field, relayed through a nested lambda), scope
//   (function body, `if` arm, loop body), record count over one store (one, two), and
//   element type (integer, struct).
//
// Axes it HOLDS FIXED, and why each is honest rather than convenient:
//
//   ⚠ The mirror only ever asserts on `Type::Reference`, so the shapes above split into
//     cells that could fail and cells that document the boundary.  Measured on the pre-fix
//     build: the eight VECTOR cells fired, and hash / sorted / index / spatial / struct /
//     text / call-result did not.  The keyed kinds and the struct are their own owners and
//     were already excused by the first half; `text` holds inline bytes and no store at all;
//     and a vector from a CALL backs onto `__ref_1: vec<int>`, a `Vector` the assert does not
//     examine.  So the keyed and call-result cells are CONTROLS for the fix's over-firing
//     side, not further instances of the defect — and the mirror staying blind to a `Vector`
//     backing local is a narrowness of `check_ref_leaks` this file does not close.
//
//   The number of captures per closure stays at one.  The verdict is per capture ATTRIBUTE
//     and the walk is per attribute, so a second capture repeats the first question rather
//     than composing with it.
//
// **A second rule meets the first at the bottom of the file, and it is here because the two
// answer the same question about one store.**  A local built from an inline record LITERAL
// mints into a `__ref_N` work-ref and is aliased to it, so the buffer's forced scope-exit free
// and the local's own free are two claims on one store.  loft#1317 made that free conditional
// where the local is RETURNED; a CAPTURED local is the opposite case, because its own free is
// already suppressed by the rule above and the buffer's is all that is left.  The two capture
// cells for it are marked, and `810-method-return-buffer` / `1181-a-captured-struct-…` are
// where the corpus scores the same boundary.
//
// TWO channels, and neither alone is enough:
//
//   1. The file COMPILES.  `check_ref_leaks` is `#[cfg(debug_assertions)]`, which
//      `[profile.dev.package.loft]` strips from every ordinary build — so this channel
//      speaks only under the nightly `-C debug-assertions=on` gate, where the pre-fix
//      build aborts with an internal compiler error before any cell runs.
//   2. Every store is FREED EXACTLY ONCE.  `wrap`'s per-test leak check scores this on an
//      ordinary build, and it is what catches the opposite mistake: a predicate that
//      over-fires suppresses a free nobody adopts, and the store is never freed at all.
//      `an_uncaptured_sibling_is_still_freed` is the cell aimed at that side.
//
// @falsified-at: hand-measured, not `falsify` — the mirror is compiled out of the ordinary
//   build the tool builds on both sides, so it reports INERT and scores nothing.  Measured
//   instead by reverting `check_ref_leaks` to its `is_captured`-only form and rebuilding
//   with `RUSTFLAGS=-C debug-assertions=on`: 8 of the 18 shapes probed abort with
//   `[check_ref_leaks] Reference variable '__vdb_1' … has no OpFreeRef`, identically on
//   `--interpret` and `--native`, and all 18 are clean with the fix restored.  The exit-leak
//   channel is scored separately and is clean in both states, which is why the compile
//   channel had to be measured at all.
//
// @falsified-at: the capture cells at the bottom score the LEAK channel instead, and were
//   measured by making the loft#1317 buffer free unconditional rather than return-only:
//   `NEVER FREED: 1 stores … kt=91 Circle×1` on `a_captured_enum_built_from_a_literal`, clean
//   with the condition restored.  The struct cell beside it stays clean either way, which is
//   the fact it is there to record.

struct R { k: text, n: integer }
struct Cell3 { x: integer, y: integer, n: integer }
struct Counter { hits: integer }
struct Holder { f: fn() -> integer }
// Both variants declare `size`, so the capture reads one shared slot and the
// `variant-field-unchecked` warning stays quiet — the subject here is the store, not the tag.
enum Shape { Circle { size: integer }, Square { size: integer } }

// --- The defect: a capture whose store lives in a BACKING local -------------------------

fn a_kept_vector_capture() {
  v = [7, 2, 3];
  total = fn() -> integer { s = 0; for e in v { s += e; } s };
  assert(total() == 12, "vector: {total()}");
}

fn a_kept_vector_of_struct_capture() {
  v = [R { k: "a", n: 7 }, R { k: "b", n: 2 }];
  total = fn() -> integer { s = 0; for e in v { s += e.n; } s };
  assert(total() == 9, "vector<struct>: {total()}");
}

fn a_vector_grown_after_it_is_declared_empty() {
  v: vector<integer> = [];
  v += [7, 2, 3];
  total = fn() -> integer { s = 0; for e in v { s += e; } s };
  assert(total() == 12, "empty then appended: {total()}");
}

fn a_capture_inside_an_if_arm() {
  if true {
    v = [7, 2, 3];
    total = fn() -> integer { s = 0; for e in v { s += e; } s };
    assert(total() == 12, "if arm: {total()}");
  }
}

fn a_capture_minted_once_per_loop_iteration() {
  tot = 0;
  for i in 0..3 {
    v = [i, 2, 3];
    total = fn() -> integer { s = 0; for e in v { s += e; } s };
    tot += total();
  }
  assert(tot == 18, "loop body: {tot}");
}

fn a_capture_held_in_a_struct_field() {
  v = [7, 2, 3];
  h = Holder { f: fn() -> integer { s = 0; for e in v { s += e; } s } };
  assert(h.f() == 12, "struct field: {h.f()}");
}

// A lambda inside a lambda RELAYS the capture outward — the inner record points at a store
// the outer frame's binding names, so only one of them may claim the free (loft#1236).
fn a_capture_relayed_through_a_nested_lambda() {
  v = [7, 2, 3];
  outer = fn() -> integer {
    inner = fn() -> integer { s = 0; for e in v { s += e; } s };
    inner()
  };
  assert(outer() == 12, "nested relay: {outer()}");
}

fn mk_total() -> fn() -> integer {
  v = [7, 2, 3];
  fn() -> integer { s = 0; for e in v { s += e; } s }
}

// The escaping fate of the same shape: the frame that minted the store is GONE by the time
// the closure reads it, so the record is the only possible owner (loft#1308).
fn a_returned_closure_keeps_the_backing_store() {
  g = mk_total();
  assert(g() == 12, "first call: {g()}");
  assert(g() == 12, "and the second reads the same store: {g()}");
}

// --- Controls: shapes that own their store directly, and must stay untouched -------------

fn a_kept_hash_capture() {
  h: hash<R[k]> = [R { k: "a", n: 7 }, R { k: "b", n: 2 }];
  total = fn() -> integer { s = 0; for e in h { s += e.n; } s };
  assert(total() == 9, "hash: {total()}");
}

fn a_kept_sorted_capture() {
  so: sorted<R[k]> = [R { k: "b", n: 2 }, R { k: "a", n: 7 }];
  total = fn() -> integer { s = 0; for e in so { s += e.n; } s };
  assert(total() == 9, "sorted: {total()}");
}

fn a_kept_index_capture() {
  ix: index<R[n]> = [R { k: "b", n: 2 }, R { k: "a", n: 7 }];
  total = fn() -> integer { s = 0; for e in ix { s += e.n; } s };
  assert(total() == 9, "index: {total()}");
}

fn a_kept_spatial_capture() {
  sp: spatial<Cell3[x,y]> = [Cell3 { x: 1, y: 1, n: 7 }, Cell3 { x: 2, y: 2, n: 2 }];
  total = fn() -> integer { s = 0; for e in sp { s += e.n; } s };
  assert(total() == 9, "spatial: {total()}");
}

fn a_kept_struct_capture() {
  c = Counter { hits: 0 };
  bump = fn() { c.hits += 5; };
  bump();
  assert(c.hits == 5, "struct: {c.hits}");
}

// A text capture is copied inline and holds no store, so there is nothing to adopt and
// nothing to suppress — the attribute carries no DbRef for the cascade to follow.
fn a_kept_text_capture() {
  t = "hello";
  read = fn() -> integer { len(t) };
  assert(read() == 5, "text: {read()}");
}

// Takes a parameter so the suite does not collect it as a cell of its own: a function that
// asserts nothing passes for the wrong reason.
fn src3(base: integer) -> vector<integer> { [base, 2, 3] }

// The backing local here is `__ref_1: vec<int>` rather than a `__vdb_N: ref(…)`, which is
// the same relationship wearing a different type.
fn a_kept_call_result_capture() {
  v = src3(7);
  total = fn() -> integer { s = 0; for e in v { s += e; } s };
  assert(total() == 12, "call result: {total()}");
}

// Where this rule meets the OTHER free the same store can attract.  A local built from an
// inline literal mints into a `__ref_N` work-ref and aliases it in, and when that local is
// RETURNED the buffer's free has to consult the alias at run time or the caller reads a
// released store (loft#1317).  A CAPTURED local is not returned, so it must keep the PLAIN
// free: its own frame-exit free is already suppressed because the record adopted the store,
// and a conditional buffer free then declines and strands it — measured here as a `Circle`
// nobody frees, and in `1181-a-captured-struct-is-not-the-callers-to-take.loft` as the same
// leak in the corpus.
//
// Only the record ENUM has a buffer to strand, and the struct beside it says why rather than
// leaving the asymmetry unexplained: a dense struct local is built straight into its own slot
// by `OpDatabase` and mints no buffer at all.
fn a_captured_enum_built_from_a_literal() {
  cap: Shape = Circle { size: 5 };
  read = fn() -> integer { cap.size };
  assert(read() == 5, "captured literal enum: {read()}");
}

fn a_captured_struct_built_from_a_literal() {
  c = Counter { hits: 7 };
  read = fn() -> integer { c.hits };
  assert(read() == 7, "captured literal struct: {read()}");
}

// --- The over-firing side: what must still be freed --------------------------------------

// Two records over ONE store.  Only the store's single owner may hand it over, however many
// closures point at it.
fn two_closures_over_one_vector() {
  v = [7];
  add2 = fn() { v += [2]; };
  add3 = fn() { v += [3]; };
  add2();
  add3();
  assert(len(v) == 3, "two records, one store: {len(v)}");
}

// A local vector in a frame that ALSO has a captured one.  If the predicate widened to
// "a heap local in a capturing frame", `loose`'s free would be suppressed with no record
// adopting it — and the leak check would report a store nobody freed.
fn an_uncaptured_sibling_is_still_freed() {
  kept = [7, 2, 3];
  loose = [4, 5, 6];
  total = fn() -> integer { s = 0; for e in kept { s += e; } s };
  assert(total() == 12, "captured: {total()}");
  assert(len(loose) == 3, "uncaptured sibling: {len(loose)}");
}

fn length_seen_by_a_captured_parameter(xs: vector<integer>) -> integer {
  read = fn() -> integer { len(xs) };
  read()
}

// A captured PARAMETER stays a BORROW: the caller owns that store and outlives this frame,
// so adopting it would cascade a second free onto a live store (#682) — strictly worse than
// the leak this file is about.
fn a_captured_parameter_leaves_the_caller_whole() {
  xs = [7, 2, 3];
  assert(length_seen_by_a_captured_parameter(xs) == 3, "parameter capture");
  assert(len(xs) == 3, "caller's vector intact: {len(xs)}");
  assert(xs[0] == 7 && xs[2] == 3, "with its elements: {xs[0]} {xs[2]}");
}

fn main() {
  a_kept_vector_capture();
  a_kept_vector_of_struct_capture();
  a_vector_grown_after_it_is_declared_empty();
  a_capture_inside_an_if_arm();
  a_capture_minted_once_per_loop_iteration();
  a_capture_held_in_a_struct_field();
  a_capture_relayed_through_a_nested_lambda();
  a_returned_closure_keeps_the_backing_store();
  a_kept_hash_capture();
  a_kept_sorted_capture();
  a_kept_index_capture();
  a_kept_spatial_capture();
  a_kept_struct_capture();
  a_kept_text_capture();
  a_kept_call_result_capture();
  a_captured_enum_built_from_a_literal();
  a_captured_struct_built_from_a_literal();
  two_closures_over_one_vector();
  an_uncaptured_sibling_is_still_freed();
  a_captured_parameter_leaves_the_caller_whole();
}
